> Now that nethack is getting so huge, with side quests and all, why is this 
> considered cheating? If you go and play a commercial game (such as Ultima), 
> do you have to play the whole game without saving? Because if you die, you 
> can always go back and restore that saved game... I've never heard that 
> considered cheating...

The difference is, or at least used to be, that in Ultima, the whole
point is to discover the secrets of the game.  Once you've done that,
you've won.  Whereas in nethack, like Space Invaders or any pinball
game, you have to "perform" in order to win.  With skill, strategy, and
luck you have to make it all the way to the end without dying.
Being able to restart in the middle after dying makes nethack into
a game very much like Ultima.  And the fun would last about as long.
So basically the reason is "because that's the way the game goals were
defined."

As you say, nethack is getting really complex, and is becoming more
like an Ultima-style game.  There are a lot of steps to figure out,
not to mention perform.  And it's such a loooooooooooooooonnnnnnnnggggggg
game to win.  It does seem like some sort of limited restorable saving
should be in order.  E.g., just as you finish the quest, you get one
opportunity to do a restorable save.  That would cut the game roughly
into two halves.  (Equal in terms of difficulty if not length, I
think.)

Any comments?

